 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np5-01",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Pluperfect",
   "quote": "conflixerat … pepulerat",
   "trans": "he had fought … had driven back",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The pluperfects reach back before chapter 3 ended, so the narrative picks up events that happened on the way to the Alps.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-02",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Repetition",
   "quote": "cum hoc eodem … tertio idem Scipio",
   "trans": "with this same man … for the third time this same Scipio",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Nepos keeps stressing that it is the same Roman commander, beaten again and again, which makes Hannibal's dominance seem relentless.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-03",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Historic present",
   "quote": "decernit … dimittit",
   "trans": "he fights it out … sends him away",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The switch into the present makes this second battle vivid, as if happening before the reader's eyes.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "sauciumque inde ac fugatum",
   "trans": "wounded and routed",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two participles show the consul both hurt and running, a humiliating picture of a Roman commander.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-05",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Asyndeton",
   "quote": "manum conseruit, utrosque profligavit",
   "trans": "he engaged them, he crushed them both",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two short clauses with no connective: the battle is over as soon as it begins.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-06",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "utrosque",
   "trans": "both of them",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two consuls together still cannot stop Hannibal, which raises the scale of the victory.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-07",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Contrast",
   "quote": "adeo gravi morbo afficitur oculorum, ut postea numquam dextro aeque bene usus sit",
   "trans": "he was struck by so severe an eye disease that he never again used his right eye as well",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Right in the middle of his victories, Hannibal is shown as a man suffering lasting physical harm, which makes him human.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-08",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "lecticaque ferretur",
   "trans": "was being carried in a litter",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The great general is too ill to walk or ride, which makes what follows all the more remarkable.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-09",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Contrast",
   "quote": "lecticaque ferretur, C. Flaminium consulem … occidit",
   "trans": "was carried in a litter … killed the consul Gaius Flaminius",
   "line": "",
   "body": "A sick man in a litter destroys a consul and his army: the contrast shows that Hannibal's mind, not his body, wins battles.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-10",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "insidiis circumventum",
   "trans": "surrounded by an ambush",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Hannibal wins by cunning, a theme that runs through the Life: Romans fall into his traps.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-11",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "cum delecta manu",
   "trans": "with a picked force",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Even Rome's best troops are beaten, which adds to the sense that no one can resist him.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-12",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "obviam ei venerunt duo consules",
   "trans": "the two consuls came out to meet him",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Rome sends both its consuls at once, a sign of how desperate the situation has become.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-13",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "uno proelio",
   "trans": "in a single battle",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two consular armies routed in one battle: the phrase stresses the scale of Cannae without needing to name it.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-14",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Climax",
   "quote": "Paulum consulem occidit et aliquot praeterea consulares, in his Cn. Servilium Geminum",
   "trans": "he killed the consul Paulus and several former consuls besides, among them Gnaeus Servilius Geminus",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The list of dead rises from a consul to a group of ex-consuls, so the reader sees how much of Rome's leadership died at Cannae.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-15",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Precise detail",
   "quote": "qui superiore anno fuerat consul",
   "trans": "who had been consul the year before",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Naming a man who was consul only a year earlier makes the losses concrete and personal for Roman readers.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-16",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Publius Cornelius Scipio",
   "quote": "P. Cornelio Scipione consule",
   "trans": "the consul Publius Cornelius Scipio",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Consul in 218 BC and father of Scipio Africanus, who would beat Hannibal at Zama. At the Rhône there was only a clash of cavalry patrols: Scipio arrived after Hannibal had already crossed.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np5-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Clastidium and the Po",
   "quote": "Clastidi apud Padum",
   "trans": "at Clastidium on the Po",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The battle usually called the Ticinus (218 BC), a cavalry battle near the Po, in which Scipio was badly wounded. Clastidium was a nearby town that Hannibal later took.",
   "exam": ""
