   "h": "Hannibal's victories in Italy",
   "body": "<p>In 218 BC Hannibal came down from the Alps into the Po valley. He beat Publius Cornelius Scipio in a cavalry battle at the Ticinus, near Clastidium, where Scipio was wounded, and then, in December, the combined armies of Scipio and Tiberius Sempronius Longus at the river Trebia. In spring 217 BC he crossed the Apennines and the flooded marshes of the Arno, where he lost the sight of one eye, and in June trapped the consul Gaius Flaminius at Lake Trasimene: Flaminius and most of his army were killed.</p><p>In 216 BC came Cannae in Apulia. The consuls Lucius Aemilius Paullus and Gaius Terentius Varro led the largest army Rome had ever put into the field, and Hannibal surrounded and destroyed it. Paullus died with tens of thousands of his men. It was the worst defeat in Rome's history.</p>"
